# team/system-design — worker-side persistent segment mapping cache
#
# One change on top of main@7e3d2e6: the ring worker keeps the RW mapping of
# each data segment instead of running shm_open/fchmod/fstat/ftruncate/mmap/
# madvise/munmap around every batch. The client's RO mapping was already cached;
# this is the worker's half.
#
# Same image recipe, same script, same parameters on both sides, one run at a
# time under /tmp/fpq-bench.lock. Three interleaved runs per side; the numbers
# below are the medians.
#
# command (run once per side, alternating; the script is in the repo and is
# bind-mounted so the baseline image runs the identical file):
#
#   docker build -q --target dev -t fpq-<side> -f docker/Dockerfile .
#   docker run --rm --shm-size=3g -e BENCH_GIT_REV=<side> \
#     -v $PWD/bench/dispatch/worker-map-batch.py:/src/bench/dispatch/worker-map-batch.py:ro \
#     fpq-<side> python3 /src/bench/dispatch/worker-map-batch.py 3000 100
#
# What the script does: one synchronous stream over a real table (the benchmark's
# row shape, 600k rows), 100 fetches of 3000 rows, timing ring.next_rows() and
# _client.read_rows() apart; then a second stream at 100k rows per fetch as a
# control. "worker" is the worker's turn wall time; "worker_cpu" is the worker's
# own on-CPU time from /proc/<pid>/schedstat, which excludes the process wakeup
# that wall time includes and no cache can remove.

## baseline: main@7e3d2e6, image fpq-baseline

### ENV
cpu: AMD Ryzen 7 7700 8-Core Processor
mem: 30 GiB
ghz: 3.8
cores: 8
postgres: 18.6
python: 3.13.5
sqlalchemy: 2.0.40
pyarrow: 26.0.0
adbc: 1.13.0
shape: bench
git: baseline
shm: 3.0G
### END ENV

